      "seed": 599491343,
      "version": 1774,
      "versionNonce": 984693609,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789637020518,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "System design The Rate Limiter\n\n- A rate limiter, as the name suggests, puts a limit on the number of requests a service fulfills. it throttles requests that cross the predefined limit. \n\nBelow are scenarios where the rale limits can be used \n    - Preventing resource starvation\n    - Managing policies and quotas\n    - Controlling data flow\n    - Avoiding excess costs\n\nRequirements of a Rate Limiter's Design\n\n    - Functional requirements\n\n        - To limit the number of requests a client can send to an API with a time window\n        - To make the limit of requests per window configurable.\n        - To make sure that the client gets a message (error or notification) whenever the defined threshold is crossed within a single server or combination of servers.\n    \n    - Non - functional requirements \n        \n        - Availability\n        - Low latency\n        - Scalability \n\nTypes of throttling \n\n    - Hard throttling - request will be discarded if exceeds limit\n    - Soft throttling - can handle some % of limit \n    - Elastic or dynamic throttling - if resource is available then it will serve request even if limit is exceed \n\nwhere to place the rate limit \n\n    - On the client side\n    - On the server side\n    - As middleware\n\nbuilding blocks we will use\n\n    - Database are used to store rules defined by the a service provider and metadata of users using the service.\n    - Cache are used to cache the rules and users'data for frequent access.\n    - Queues are essential for holding the incoming requests that we are allowed by the rate limiter.\n\nHigh level design \n\n        \n    \n\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "System design The Rate Limiter\n\n- A rate limiter, as the name suggests, puts a limit on the number of requests a service fulfills. it throttles requests that cross the predefined limit. \n\nBelow are scenarios where the rale limits can be used \n    - Preventing resource starvation\n    - Managing policies and quotas\n    - Controlling data flow\n    - Avoiding excess costs\n\nRequirements of a Rate Limiter's Design\n\n    - Functional requirements\n\n        - To limit the number of requests a client can send to an API with a time window\n        - To make the limit of requests per window configurable.\n        - To make sure that the client gets a message (error or notification) whenever the defined threshold is crossed within a single server or combination of servers.\n    \n    - Non - functional requirements \n        \n        - Availability\n        - Low latency\n        - Scalability \n\nTypes of throttling \n\n    - Hard throttling - request will be discarded if exceeds limit\n    - Soft throttling - can handle some % of limit \n    - Elastic or dynamic throttling - if resource is available then it will serve request even if limit is exceed \n\nwhere to place the rate limit \n\n    - On the client side\n    - On the server side\n    - As middleware\n\nbuilding blocks we will use\n\n    - Database are used to store rules defined by the a service provider and metadata of users using the service.\n    - Cache are used to cache the rules and users'data for frequent access.\n    - Queues are essential for holding the incoming requests that we are allowed by the rate limiter.\n\nHigh level design \n\n        \n    \n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
