      "seed": 1128868609,
      "version": 290,
      "versionNonce": 272748007,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789637020518,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Algorithms for rate limiting \n\n    - Token bucket algorithms \n        The Token Bucket Algorithm works like a bucket of permission slips.\n        Each request needs a token to pass.\n        Tokens are added at a steady rate, and if the bucket runs out, new requests are paused or rejected.\n        \n        It lets your system handle small bursts but keeps the average request rate under control.\n\n    - Leaky Bucket Algorithm\n        \n        The Leaky Bucket Algorithm keeps your system’s output steady.\n        It lets requests in at any speed but processes them at a fixed, controlled rate.\n        \n        If the bucket (queue) is full, new requests are dropped.\n        It’s like a faucet dripping water — slow, predictable, and steady — even if a whole bucket of water gets poured in at once.\n\n    - Fixed window counter\n\n        The Fixed Window Counter algorithm splits time into equal blocks (like 1 minute).\n        It counts how many requests each user makes in that block.\n        If the count exceeds the limit, new requests are rejected until the next window starts.\n        \n        It’s fast and simple, but can allow short bursts right at the window boundary. \n\n    - Sliding window log algorithm\n\n        The Sliding Window Log Algorithm keeps a list of timestamps for each request.\n        When a new one comes in, it deletes old ones (outside the window) and checks if the number of recent requests is under the limit.\n        \n        It’s accurate and fair.\n        But not memory-efficient for high traffic.\n\n    - Sliding window counter algorithm \n\n        The Sliding Window Counter algorithm combines simplicity and accuracy.\n        It uses the counts from the current and previous time windows and blends them based on how much time has passed —\n        creating a smooth, continuous rate limit without sharp resets.\n        \n        Prevents bursts\n        Uses little memory\n        Accurate enough for production systems\n        \n\n        \n\n        \n        \n        ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Algorithms for rate limiting \n\n    - Token bucket algorithms \n        The Token Bucket Algorithm works like a bucket of permission slips.\n        Each request needs a token to pass.\n        Tokens are added at a steady rate, and if the bucket runs out, new requests are paused or rejected.\n        \n        It lets your system handle small bursts but keeps the average request rate under control.\n\n    - Leaky Bucket Algorithm\n        \n        The Leaky Bucket Algorithm keeps your system’s output steady.\n        It lets requests in at any speed but processes them at a fixed, controlled rate.\n        \n        If the bucket (queue) is full, new requests are dropped.\n        It’s like a faucet dripping water — slow, predictable, and steady — even if a whole bucket of water gets poured in at once.\n\n    - Fixed window counter\n\n        The Fixed Window Counter algorithm splits time into equal blocks (like 1 minute).\n        It counts how many requests each user makes in that block.\n        If the count exceeds the limit, new requests are rejected until the next window starts.\n        \n        It’s fast and simple, but can allow short bursts right at the window boundary. \n\n    - Sliding window log algorithm\n\n        The Sliding Window Log Algorithm keeps a list of timestamps for each request.\n        When a new one comes in, it deletes old ones (outside the window) and checks if the number of recent requests is under the limit.\n        \n        It’s accurate and fair.\n        But not memory-efficient for high traffic.\n\n    - Sliding window counter algorithm \n\n        The Sliding Window Counter algorithm combines simplicity and accuracy.\n        It uses the counts from the current and previous time windows and blends them based on how much time has passed —\n        creating a smooth, continuous rate limit without sharp resets.\n        \n        Prevents bursts\n        Uses little memory\n        Accurate enough for production systems\n        \n\n        \n\n        \n        \n        ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "G31pnTGjwyXfA39lyQeqo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 371,
      "y": -507.5,
      "width": 973.3792114257812,
      "height": 1175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1M",
